  "description": "A parametric DOWNLIGHT on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nDROP IT ON A NODE at the position you want the spot. This is the object the plan's six recessed spots need; `light-bulb-with-math` mounts a lamp, it does not cut a ceiling.\n\nTRIM STYLE MOVES TWO PLANES AND NOTHING ELSE. Flush puts the lens at the soffit, Recessed drops it half an aperture up inside, Surface hangs the whole housing below, Gimbal aims it. Each is one flag, and the lens plane, the housing plane and the beam offset are written in terms of them, so there is no fourth body to keep in step.\n\nTHE BEAM IS REPORTED AS A FOOTPRINT, NOT MODELLED AS A CONE. `_br = ceilingHeight × tan(beamAngle / 2)` is the radius the cone actually makes at the floor, drawn as a plan circle and reported as a diameter. A tilted gimbal shifts that circle by `ceilingHeight × tan(tilt)` along the host's own axis — so the circle is where the light really lands, which is the thing worth drawing.\n\nILLUMINANCE FALLS OUT OF THE SAME TWO NUMBERS: lumens over the beam's floor area. It is approximate on purpose — it is a sanity figure for spacing, not a photometric calculation.\n\nHOUSING AND LENS ARE TUBE ROWS and the trim is a RING row, so the circle is resolved by the renderer at its own segment count rather than faceted here.\n\nDIMENSIONS AT THE DEFAULTS: a 75 mm aperture in a 90 mm cutout at 3.124 m, a 36° beam landing 2.03 m across, 650 lm, about 201 lx, suggested spacing 4.69 m.\n\nWHAT THIS CANNOT DO. It does not cut the ceiling — `cutoutDia` is a reported number for whoever does. No light is cast; there is no IES distribution, and the illuminance figure assumes an even beam.",
